{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1551911-mummy-exalted-crossover","id":1551911,"title":"Mummy/Exalted Crossover","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-06-29T02:53:21-07:00","id":1551911,"title":"Mummy/Exalted Crossover","content":"<span style=\"font-size:26px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>A Revised Mummy Setting</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">I know I\u2019m not alone in regarding Mummy 2<sup>nd</sup> Edition as the highpoint of the Mummy sourcebooks line. That said, some of its mechanics were either clunky or not very fleshed out. 3<sup>rd</sup> Edition fixed most of these issues, but at the same time it threw out the baby with the bath water by trashing the setting almost entirely. Now you had to play as someone with a serious mental or moral flaw and then have your own personal Jiminy Cricket stuffed into your head while dealing with the constant scrutiny of 42 Big Brothers who were eager to dissect your every word and deed and gleefully describe all the ways you were always failing to live up to their standards. (Just when you thought you\u2019d finally escaped your abusive nagging parents\u2026)</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Another issue was the Spell of Life being <b><i>far</i></b> too available in the world and for far too long. Seriously, this thing would have been the hottest spell in the history of humanity and <b><i>every</i></b> magician would have been cheerfully willing to commit murder to get ahold of it. The end result would have been <b><i>legions</i></b> of mummies wandering around the world by now, which isn\u2019t desirable as they were designed as this niche addition to the World of Darkness and there weren\u2019t all that many of them. So, knowledge of the spell <b><i>and</i></b> the ability to successfully cast it needed to be locked down. That said, these restrictions still needed to allow for the possibility of new mummies being created in the modern age.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">And finally, with the changes I\u2019d be making the Cabiri couldn\u2019t realistically exist as there was no written version of the Spell of Life for them to find and copy. However, I\u2019d recently discovered World of Darkness: Immortals and this struck me as the perfect crossover. The Purified are clearly an homage to the World of Darkness mummies, though their form of immortality is decidedly inferior. But, that makes them the perfect choice for a \u201cLesser Rite\u201d companion to the Great Rite.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Here\u2019s an overview of the setting and revisions for the Reborn:</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">This version of Mummy could be best described as \u201c3<sup>rd</sup> Edition going on 2<sup>nd</sup> Edition.\u201d The setting is from 2<sup>nd</sup> Edition, but the rules, Backgrounds, Merits, and Flaws are from 3<sup>rd</sup> Edition. So, there are no \u201csoul fragments\u201d flying about looking for hosts, no Judges of Ma\u2019at constantly looking over your shoulder, and Amenti is still very much intact in the Underworld. In addition, the newer version of the Spell of Life from 3<sup>rd</sup> Edition was retconned as having been that way since the beginning.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">There are no <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Wu_T%27ian\" target=\"_blank\">Wu T\u2019ian</a> and no <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Teomallki\" target=\"_blank\">Teomallki</a>. The <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Cult_of_Isis\" target=\"_blank\">Cult of Isis</a> have the only magicians capable of successfully casting the <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Spell_of_Life\" target=\"_blank\">Spell of Life</a> upon mortals to turn them into mummies.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">There are no <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Bane_Mummies\" target=\"_blank\">Bane Mummies</a>, either, as Set would not have been able to cast the Spell of Life without the consent of Cytheria (see below). His followers must make do with creating <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Asekh-sen\" target=\"_blank\">Asekh-sen</a> as shock troops instead.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Upon their First Death, the new mummy <b><i>does</i></b> emerge in the <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Underworld_(WOD)\" target=\"_blank\">Underworld</a>, enveloped in a <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Caul_(WTO)\" target=\"_blank\">Caul</a> like any other newly-dead Wraith, but they\u2019re strangely ignored by the inhabitants of the <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Shadowlands_(WOD)\" target=\"_blank\">Shadowlands</a> until they're drawn back to their body by the Great Rite. Some mummy scholars have posited that Anubis protects them during this vulnerable time, but this is mere speculation.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Upon their First Rebirth, the new mummy\u2019s body is returned to the peak of health and fitness and appears to be in its early twenties.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The Shemsu-Heru are those mummies who swear fealty to Horus. The Ishmaelites are those mummies who <b><i>were</i></b> members of the Shemsu-Heru but later defected, or mummies created in later centuries who declined to join his crusade. For his part, Horus despises the Ishmaelites and sees little difference between those who abandoned his cause and those who never joined it in the first place; he has a very \u201cyou\u2019re either with me or against me\u201d attitude.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Mummies aren\u2019t bound to the <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Web_of_Faith\" target=\"_blank\">Web of Faith</a> and there\u2019s no such thing as semektet (a weakened state for 3<sup>rd</sup> Edition mummies when they had no Sekhem remaining).</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Mummies regain one point of Sekhem each day at dawn along with an extra point if they\u2019re within the <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Lands_of_Faith\" target=\"_blank\">Lands of Faith</a>. If within a <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Node_(MTAs)\" target=\"_blank\">Node</a> / <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Caern\" target=\"_blank\">Caern</a> / Dragon Nest, they can spend a full day there meditating to recover Sekhem; the roll is Wits + Meditation (difficulty 6).</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The Balance Trait no longer exists. Use the new Sahu Trait instead for any rolls that would involve Balance in canon (see below).</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Mummies gain the following Supernatural Merits for free:</span></li> </ul><ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Blessing of Ra (7 points \u2013 Mummy: The Resurrection Players Guide, p. 115; this Merit has been altered, see below for details)</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Burning Aura (1 point \u2013 M20 The Book of Secrets, pp. 68)</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Gift of Ma\u2019at (3 points \u2013 Mummy: The Resurrection Players Guide, p. 115; this Merit has been altered, see below for details)</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Immunity to Disease (3 points \u2013 Mummy: The Resurrection Players Guide, p. 115)</span>\u200b</li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Immunity to Poisons (3 points \u2013 Mummy: The Resurrection Players Guide, p. 115)</span>\u200b</li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Spark of Life (5 points \u2013 M20 The Book of Secrets, pp. 78)</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Unbondable (2 points; this Merit has been altered, see below for details)</span></li> </ol><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">\u200b<span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The reason for this package of Merits is a replacement of sorts for the Judges of Ma\u2019at. Instead of a stable of gods constantly judging you in an effort to keep you on the straight and narrow, now your very nature ensures that you can\u2019t join up with the Forces of Darkness\u2122. Vampires and monsters such as Banes, Fomori, etc. will always feel unnatural and <b><i>wrong</i></b> to you. And if they even come near you, your Blessing of Ra will instantly kick in, lashing out with a Forbiddance attempt. You\u2019ll certainly never be welcome at Elysium. Like it not, you\u2019re on the side of the angels, and with your Burning Aura Merit, you can\u2019t really hide, either. (The Blessing of Ra and Spark of Life Merits make your aura even harder to hide on top of that.)</span></div><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Contrary to 3<sup>rd</sup> Edition canon, mummies can\u2019t just conveniently opt out of their immortality at the drop of a hat via \u201cno will to live\u201d or \u201cself-destruction\u201d (as per Mummy 3<sup>rd</sup> Edition, p. 137). The Spell of Life is pretty much a one-way trip. You\u2019re going to be around for a long, long time\u2026</span></li> </ul><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">And for the Cabiri:</span><ul><li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">These immortals were created via the Elixir of Life, whose effects qualify as a second-rate version of the Spell of Life. This \u201cLesser Rite\u201d transforms mortal humans into creatures that superficially resemble true mummies, but their version of immorality is not as resilient.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The Elixir of Life is a highly-advanced potion, requiring either Alchemy 6 and Enchantment 6, or Alchemy 6 and Ephemera 6 to make. It also requires numerous rare and expensive ingredients, including several deadly toxins and blood from the person who will drink it. Actually brewing it requires a full lunar month, during which time the alchemist is more-or-less confined to their laboratory as they monitor the process every waking moment. The person who will drink it does not need to be the person who makes it, but they\u2019re literally trusting their life to someone else if they\u2019re not the elixir\u2019s creator.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Upon drinking, the elixir takes one minute to suffuse itself within the drinker\u2019s body, then takes effect as a Rating 7 Toxin. (See the Toxin rules in M20, pp. 441-442.) Barring extraordinary measures, this will see the drinker dead in short order. Over the next three days, their mind and soul will slowly complete the transformation into an immortal spirit within the <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Penumbra\" target=\"_blank\">Penumbra</a>. Once this is complete, the new immortal must find their way back across the <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Gauntlet_(WOD)\" target=\"_blank\">Gauntlet</a> to their body. This requires an expenditure of Willpower and/or Gnosis points equal to the local Gauntlet rating (see M20, p. 484). If this is not feasible, they must travel the Penumbra to find a Node, Caern, Shallowing, Haunt or other location where the Gauntlet is thinner and try again. Their spirit form is assumed to have Rage equal to their Strength and Gnosis equal to their Intelligence. They can regain spent Gnosis points by meditating quietly for an hour with Wits + Meditation roll (difficulty 8).</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Back on Earth, with a successful Perception + Awareness roll (difficulty 6), they can follow a silvery cord back to their body\u2019s location; this is their immortal spirit\u2019s bond to their earthly body. In their absence, their body does not rot and requires neither food nor water. If examined by a doctor, it would appear to have an exceptionally faint and slow heartbeat and to be in an exceptionally deep coma. Once the drinker manages to return to their body, it immediately comes back to life and during this process returns to the peak of health and fitness in its early twenties. From that point on, their body heals more rapidly than normal and completely ceases to age. They\u2019re also immune to diseases and poisons.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The new immortal retains the ability to use Sorcery, but loses the potential to Awaken. If already Awakened, they lose that status and are now treated as a Sorcerer instead. They\u2019re also unaffected by vampire blood and thus can\u2019t be Embraced. If they had the Ghoul, Garou Kinfolk, or Kinain templates, those are lost as well.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Their newly immortal body isn\u2019t any more durable than a normal human\u2019s, and can die just as easily. If and when this happens, they\u2019ll be forced out of it into their spirit form and find themselves once again back in the Penumbra. This is a vulnerable time for them, as in this state they can meet Final Death. If slain in the same manner as other spirits \u2013 losing all of their Essence and then being harvested for Gnosis \u2013 that\u2019s a permanent end for them. In addition, if their physical body is somehow completely destroyed, they\u2019ll be trapped in the Umbra as a spirit. However, if they can once again make their way back to Earth and then to their body, they can repair it by spending Willpower and/or Gnosis points to heal Health Levels on a 1-for-1 basis.</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The requirement of achieving arch-mastery of <b><i>two</i></b> Paths is the reason there aren\u2019t more Cabiri in the world, as Sorcerers with that powerset don't exactly grow on trees. Awakened Mages could pull this off with Prime 5 and Spirit 5, but that would be their last act as an Awakened Mage so even those masters of magic that know of this path aren't keen utilizing it to prolong their lives.</span></li> </ul><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:26px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Prehistory \u2013 The Exalted Age</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Cytherea</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">In the wake of the <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Primordial_War\" target=\"_blank\">Primordial War</a>, having narrowly escaped the fate of the <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Neverborn_(AOS)\" target=\"_blank\">Neverborn</a> or the <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Yozi\" target=\"_blank\">Yozi</a>, she retreated to hidden realms of her own creation in the <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Wyld_(AOS)\" target=\"_blank\">Wyld</a>. Throughout the rest of the history of <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Creation\" target=\"_blank\">Creation</a> she periodically interfered in the schemes of the <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/God_(AOS)\" target=\"_blank\">Gods</a> and Exalted alike, but always from behind the scenes. She continued her self-imposed exile into the age of World of Darkness, along with her predilection for interference from the shadows.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">In the early days of the land of Kemet, she foresaw their evolution into the great empire of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Egypt\" target=\"_blank\">Ancient Egypt</a> and was moved to guide their development as their coming civilization reminded her of the golden age of Creation. In the guise of Thoth, she taught Isis and Nephthys the secrets of magic and later guided Isis into crafting and casting the Spell of Life to help purge the forces of <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Apophis\" target=\"_blank\">Apophis</a> from the land. Millennia later, it still only by her will that High Priests and Priestesses of the Cult of Isis can enact the Great Rite upon mortals to transform them into the Reborn; when the faithful pray to Isis, it is she who hears their prayers. She crafted the Reborn as replacement of sorts for the Exalted, but instead of creating nigh-invincible powerhouses she focused on creating agents whose greatest strength was their ability to ultimately ignore any defeat and return to their mission time after time. This approach, she feels, has avoided many of the problems that arose with the larger-than-life Exalted whose great levels of power ultimately contributed to their corruption and the fall of Creation.</span><br /> \u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:26px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Prehistory \u2013 Ancient Egypt</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>The History of the Reborn</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">This history of the ancient world and how mummies came to be is how it was described in Mummy 1<sup>st</sup> Edition (pp. 58-65) and Mummy 2<sup>nd</sup> Edition (pp. 16-21). This story was also neatly recounted by Lazar of Stygia <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IujMRmG4lPQ\" target=\"_blank\">here</a> on YouTube.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>The Creation of the Artifacts of Ma\u2019at</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">In the waning days of the Ancient Egyptian empire, beset by foreign forces that were increasingly eroding their culture and sovereignty, the priesthood of Isis and those remaining priesthoods of the other old gods assembled at a hidden temple in a last great conclave. Their civilization\u2019s end was likely at hand, but their crusade against Apophis remained. It was paramount that the Reborn would continue to have support and that new worthy Reborn would continue to be justly selected in the ages to come.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">For the first concern, the most essential priests of Isis would entrench and go underground, prioritizing the security of their most important temples, the ones where the Great Rite could be enacted. These would be obscured and hidden to the best of the Isis priesthood\u2019s ability while their allies in the other priesthoods would carefully work to erase any evidence that they had ever existed at all.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">But for the problem of selecting new Reborn, they debated for some time and finally decided on a novel solution to a very long-term issue. The priests of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maat\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u2019at</a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shai\" target=\"_blank\">Shai</a> would work in conjunction with the priests of Isis to create seven sacred ushabti. Into these would be bound seven wise and loyal spirits who would take on the duty of selecting worthy candidates. The magics enchanting them would both protect them from harm and see to their serendipitous movement around the world as fate directed them to those whom they would evaluate. Unbeknownst to them, Cytherea quietly guided their efforts as she approved of their plan.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">This great effort took over a year and when it was at last complete the priests of Ma\u2019at and Shai departed, taking with them the ushabti to quietly send out into the world. Long after all of them had departed the Earth, these immortal agents would continue to scrutinize humanity to determine those who would join the ranks the Reborn. And thus it has been for millennia.</span><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:26px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Prehistory \u2013 Ancient Greece</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>The History of the Cabiri</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">In the sixth century B.C., a Greek magus traveling in Samothrace met with a man named Cabirus, who claimed to be an immortal. He credited his immortality to a powerful rite performed by the high priest of an Egyptian cult, and claimed to know the secret of making others like himself. The magus, versed in alchemic and necromantic magicks, recorded all of the knowledge of the rite imparted to him by Cabirus and then returned home to Thessalonica. He eventually became the first \u201clesser immortal.\u201d</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">His notes came to be known as <i>The Secret Writings of Cabirus</i> and would eventually attain the status of the most sought-after grimoire of the ancient world. Horus was greatly displeased when he learned of it, and what happened next was a seemingly endless cycle. A copy of the <i>Writings</i> would languish for years in some remote location, be rediscovered by some mystery cult, and eventually a new immortal would emerge. Agents of Horus would learn of this and then track down the copy and destroy it (along with the new immortal, if they could find them). But it was never the last copy, no matter how many times they tried. Horus persists in his seemingly-futile crusade to expunge every last copy, but with the advent of the Information Age this has become a Sisyphean endeavor.</span><br /> \u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:26px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Cosmology</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Amenti \u2013 The Dark Kingdom of Sand</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Contrary to some rumors, Amenti, the Dark Kingdom of Sand, is still very much intact the Underworld and provides a place of refuge for all of the Reborn in between their lives on Earth. It remained untouched by the Oblivion Storm [this was the Avatar Storm in WoD canon] as it\u2019s protected by Cytherea (see above) in her various guises as the gods of Ancient Egypt. Normally, only the Reborn may enter Amenti and even <b><i>finding</i></b> it in the Underworld is impossible for most Wraiths. But, from time to time some Wraiths or other Umbral travelers of good character have been permitted find it and enter this unique realm. (The bonded familiars of mummies are always welcome to accompany their masters into Amenti.)</span><br /> \u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:26px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Organizations</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>The Cult of Isis</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The priesthood of Isis have (barely) survived into the modern era and maintain a small network of hidden underground temples throughout the Middle East. This is a testament to both their tenacity and that of their hidden patron, Cytherea, a surviving Primordial from a long-lost age (see above). She\u2019s assumed the guise of Isis for millennia now and is the one answering their prayers. It is only by her will that the Spell of Life can successfully be cast, as even her high priests and priestesses don\u2019t realize that this epic magic is beyond mortal ken. All of their surviving temples are at least rating 5 Nodes, which is a necessary component of the Great Rite. The spell itself is actually an invocation to plead for her intercession and the conduit by which that can be enacted. As such, these hidden temples have heavy magical wards and a plethora of modern defenses as well; local officials are also well-bribed to stay out of their affairs.</span><br /> \u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:26px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Notable Relics</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>The Artifacts of Ma\u2019at</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Millennia ago, these seven ushabti were crafted and sent out into the world where, through seeming fate and happenstance, they end up in the possession of those who are good candidates to become one of the Reborn. Each ushabti is inhabited by an ancient and wise spirit who spends years evaluating a given candidate. During this time, the spirit both tutors and subtly tests them to evaluate their character and abilities. If the candidate is deemed acceptable, they\u2019re directed to one of the hidden temples of the Cult of Isis within the Lands of Faith. If they fail the evaluation, the ushabti disappears back into the world in search of the next candidate. Physically, each ushabti is about 10\u201d tall, delicately carved from hard stone and exquisitely painted. They are all of the goddess Ma\u2019at, though each portrays a differently dressed and posed version of the deity.</span><br /> \u200b<br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:26px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>New and Updated Advantages</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">NOTE: These traits apply only to the Reborn, not the Cabiri.</span><br /> \u200b<br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Backgrounds</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><u>Ba</u></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>X</b> Your lifespan on Earth is only 50 years and you\u2019ll then spend a full year rejuvenating in </span><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Amenti before your resurrection. You have no extra dice towards your resurrection roll (see Mummy: The Resurrection, pp. 136-140).</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf</b> Your lifespan is 60 years, you\u2019ll spend 9 months in Amenti, and you have +1 resurrection die.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf</b> Your lifespan is 70 years, you\u2019ll spend 7 months in Amenti, and you have +2 resurrection dice.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</b> Your lifespan is 80 years, you\u2019ll spend 5 months in Amenti, and you have +3 resurrection dice.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</b> Your lifespan is 90 years, you\u2019ll spend 3 months in Amenti, and you have +4 resurrection dice.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</b> Your lifespan is 100 years, you\u2019ll spend 1 month in Amenti, and you have +5 resurrection dice.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">REFERENCE: Mummy: The Resurrection, p. 64</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><u>Cult</u></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">You have a cult of followers who revere you as a religious figure. Once per month, they can gather to perform a group ritual to aid in your resurrection if you are dead. A sorcerer must conduct this ritual, and they must have Magery equal to your Cult Background. If they fail to meet this standard, they can\u2019t make use of the full cult (or the cult is of insufficient size). Each member suffers a level of Lethal damage, but in exchange for their sacrifice you require less time in Amenti before your resurrection. If their sacrifice clears your remaining time required, you can elect to resurrect then and there.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>X</b> You have no cult and thus only your Ba Background determines how soon you can resurrect.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf</b> Tiny Cult of 3-7 people. Your time in Amenti is lessened by a week.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf</b> Small Cult of 8-12 people. Your time in Amenti is lessened by two weeks.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</b> Moderate Cult of 13-17 people. Your time in Amenti is lessened by three weeks.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</b> Large Cult of 18-22 people. Your time in Amenti is lessened by four weeks.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</b> Huge Cult of 23-30+ people. Your time in Amenti is lessened by five weeks.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">REFERENCE: This is a custom Background. | M20, p. 310</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><u>Vessel</u></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf</b> The vessel can hold 3 Sekhem points.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf</b> The vessel can hold 6 Sekhem points.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</b> The vessel can hold 9 Sekhem points.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</b> The vessel can hold 12 Sekhem points.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</b> The vessel can hold 15 Sekhem points.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u25cf</b> The vessel can hold 18 Sekhem points.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u25cf\u25cf</b> The vessel can hold 21 Sekhem points.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</b> The vessel can hold 24 Sekhem points.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</b> The vessel can hold 27 Sekhem points.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</b> The vessel can hold 30 Sekhem points.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">REFERENCE: Mummy: The Resurrection, p. 67</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Merits</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><u>Blessing of Ra (7 points)</u></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Imbued with holy wrath, you can easily turn away the foul minions of Apophis. Reduce by two the difficulty of any Forbiddance attempt (Mummy: The Resurrection, p. 142). Creatures successfully banished by you suffer one level of aggravated damage as your righteousness sears their tainted soul. You also make reflexive Forbiddance attempts whenever a malign entity even comes near you. Since this reaction is automatic, it doesn\u2019t require an action. In addition, you have a golden nimbus surrounding your aura.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">REFERENCE Mummy: The Resurrection Players Guide, pp. 115</span><br /> \u200b<br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><u>Effortless Resurrection (7 points)</u></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Unlike most of the Reborn, you needn\u2019t sacrifice a point of permanent Sekhem or Willpower in order to resurrect yourself. However, if you die yet again and attempt to resurrect yourself within a year of your last resurrection, you must spend permanent Sekhem or Willpower as any other mummy would.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">REFERENCE: This is a custom Merit.</span><br /> \u200b<br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><u>Gift of Ma\u2019at (3 points)</u></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Attuned to the ways of cosmic balance, you can naturally sense the presence of those who serve or oppose the cosmic order. Whenever anyone with a Corruption rating enters the range of your natural senses, your Storyteller should secretly roll your Humanity (difficulty 4). Success reveals the presence of an unrighteous being and you will know which being that is. Any such beings feel unnatural and <b><i>wrong</i></b> to your senses.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">NOTE: All vampires have a Corruption rating of at least 1, which is very likely to increase as they age and their Humanity score decreases.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">REFERENCE Mummy: The Resurrection Players Guide, pp. 115, 198-213</span><br /> \u200b<br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><u>Resilient Khat (5 points)</u></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Even if your khat is <b><i>totally</i></b> destroyed, it will slowly reform as a pile of ashes in the nearest safe and likely-out-of-sight location from the point of its destruction. This takes a lunar month and even then your body has a Health Level of Dust/Ash, so resurrection requires six successes. However, even if your body was blasted to atoms in a nuclear explosion, you can still come back.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">REFERENCE: This is a custom Merit and was priced as per the Supernatural Merit: Unity of Being.</span><br /> \u200b<br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><u>Unbondable (2 points)</u></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">This rather infamous Merit has been extensively revamped\u2026pun intended. If you take this Merit, you\u2019re effectively opting out of Vampirism and Ghouldom. You\u2019re simply unaffected by vampire blood. You can\u2019t be Embraced and you can\u2019t become a Ghoul, either. The reduced cost reflects your inability to \u201cdouble-dip\u201d on powers, gaining access to vampiric Disciplines in addition to any other supernatural powers you might have.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">REFERENCE: M20 The Book of Secrets, pp. 75</span><br /> \u200b<br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Other Traits</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table_MsoTableGrid\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Trait</b></span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Character Creation</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Freebie Cost</b></span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Later Improvement</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>XP Cost</b></span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Sahu</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">5 per dot</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">new rating * 10</span></td> </tr> </table></div> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">This trait is effectively Arete for mummies, as it governs the maximum level of magic they can wield and how much Sekhem they can store within themselves. Unlike Arete, however, it requires no tests or rituals to progress to higher levels (just XP).</span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode__table--wrapper text_table_\"><table class=\"b-bbcode__table text_table_MsoTableGrid\"><tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Sahu</b></span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Hekau</b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Path Maximum</b></span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Sekhem Stored/Spent</b></span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">1</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">1</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">2</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">2</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">3</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">3</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">4</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">4</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">5</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">5</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u25cf</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">6</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">6</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u25cf\u25cf</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">7</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">7</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">8</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">8</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">9</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">9</span></td> </tr> <tr valign=\"top\" class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_tr\"><td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf \u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">10</span></td> <td class=\"text_table_MsoTableGrid_td\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">10</span></td> </tr> </table></div> <br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:26px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Hekau</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>The Spell of Life</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">This epic spell can\u2019t be cast by just any magician anytime they please. It must be cast by a high priest(ess) of the Cult of Isis, and in a properly sanctified temple located within the Web of Faith. It will also only work for those mortals who have been selected by one of the <i>Artifacts of Ma\u2019at</i>.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Sorcerer Paths</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The M20 Paths of Magic available to mortal sorcerers can be learned by mummies and adapted into Hekau Paths. Some of these map fairly directly (Alchemy to Alchemy, and Enchantment to Amulets), but others would be newly-developed Hekau Paths beyond the traditional six that mummies are more likely to be exposed to.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:20px\"><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><b>Rituals</b></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\"><u>Effigy</u></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf to \u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf [RELIC]: These spells require the creation of a permanent figurine that must be stored in the mummy\u2019s Tomb in order to accompany them into the Underworld.</span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf\u25cf KHAT ANCHOR: In the event of your death, this statuette (presumably safe in your Tomb) teleports your khat to its location. This prevents your body from being lost (and possibly buried or cremated) before your spirit returns to Earth.</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28819,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28819-arcanum-scholar","user":"Arcanum Scholar","date":". 07-06-2025, 04:23 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28819-arcanum-scholar","name":"Arcanum Scholar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-07T07:09:09-07:00","id":1552141,"title":"","content":"I have been looking at doing something with the Purified for a CofD like setting, and heard it was inspired by the WoD Mummy line, so this is great considering i can't get my hands on Mummy.<br /> <br /> Can you explain what the theme of the game is? How you decided on those? Or anything else which went into you design goals?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-07T14:20:05-07:00","id":1552166,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>orathaic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1551911-mummy-exalted-crossover?p=1552141#post1552141\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have been looking at doing something with the Purified for a CofD like setting, and heard it was inspired by the WoD Mummy line, so this is great considering i can't get my hands on Mummy.<br /> <br /> Can you explain what the theme of the game is? How you decided on those? Or anything else which went into you design goals?</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Well, this is a bit of a story. I've been gaming the World of Darkness on and off since the 1990s. Mummy was a niche setting addition, but it always fascinated me. Think WoD Sorcerer, but they can't die, at least not permanently. They\u2019re the Energizer Bunnies of the World of Darkness \u2013 they just keep going and going and going\u2026</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">The theme of Mummy? That\u2019s up for a bit of debate. They\u2019ve been called a good character for a mixed supernaturals party (see <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GFpn1F0J4Eg\" target=\"_blank\">this video</a> from Mr. Welch). Alternately, you can run their canon description straight, and then in that case their schtick is \u201cthey\u2019re the good guys in the World of Darkness.\u201d All the other game lines are gloom-and-doom, but they\u2019re supposed to be hope for a better future. That alone makes them stand out, and considering that I\u2019ve always been fascinated by Ancient Egypt, that sealed the deal for me and I found myself coming back to them over the years.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">As for my design goals, that\u2019s also a bit of a story. Most Mummy fans I\u2019ve known have liked 1<sup>st</sup> and 2<sup>nd</sup> Edition better than 3<sup>rd</sup> (Mummy: The Resurrection). This is because they threw out the baby with the bathwater when they updated the setting for 3<sup>rd</sup> and basically got rid of a lot of what made Mummy fun in the first place. Going from playing an immortal sorcerer to playing some schmuck with needs-therapy-level of issues who\u2019s had their own personal \u201cspirit guide\u201d shoved into their head while also now having to deal with 42 nagging parents who are always eager to tell you all of the ways you continue disappoint them\u2026yeah, that was a bit of a letdown. That said, the <b><i>mechanics</i></b> of 3<sup>rd</sup> Edition were a welcome facelift from 2<sup>nd</sup> Edition. So, what to do?</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">This was my attempt to take the fluff of 2<sup>nd</sup> Edition and mix it with the crunch of 3<sup>rd</sup> Edition. In the process, I also wanted to address what I saw as a huge plot hole for Mummy. Namely, the availability the Spell of Life, which is what transforms regular humans into mummies. If you go by canon, it seems that nearly any magician can grab a copy and promptly achieve immortality, but for some reason there just weren\u2019t a lot of mummies wandering around the world. This boggles the mind. A spell of <b><i>guaranteed immortality</i></b> would be the hottest piece of magic in all of history and there should be <b><i>millions</i></b> of mummies wandering around by this point!</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Access to that spell needed to be locked down, but at the same time I needed to leave wiggle room for players in the modern world to create new mummy characters. My solution was to retcon the Spell of Life as \u201cepic magic\u201d beyond the ken of mortal magicians. Even if you\u2019re a master magician, you can\u2019t just whip out this spell and become a mummy. Instead, only duly sanctified priests of the old gods could successfully cast it, and they would only do so for those mortals they found worthy. The Cult of Isis is about the only Ancient Egyptian priesthood still around in the modern World of Darkness (and Isis is both a goddess of healing and magic) so they were my designated group to control access to the Spell of Life. And as a further access control, it could only be successfully cast in one of their temples within the Web of Faith.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">All well and good, but, upon further reflection, I had another problem. There weren\u2019t really any gods hanging around anymore to actually facilitate this. The closest thing the modern World of Darkness has to all-powerful gods would probably be the <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Celestine_(WOD)\" target=\"_blank\">Celestines</a>, but they\u2019re\u2026not really what I needed here. I needed some old gods (or at least <b><i>one</i></b> old god) who was still hanging about. That got me thinking, while Exalted isn\u2019t canonically the long-lost past of the World of Darkness universe (though it was <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/exalted/comments/li0rfo/found_an_old_exalted_ad_from_before_the_game_was/\" target=\"_blank\">originally going to be</a>), many fans still run with that, including me. Doing some digging, I found a <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Cytherea\" target=\"_blank\">Primordial</a> who could conceivably fit the bill, with just a bit of backstory fudging.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">And this actually ended up being a better concept than I intended. Immortal champions created by a goddess to be her agents on Earth? Sounds a lot like the Exalted themselves, so I ran with the idea. The mummies being \u201cExalted Lite\u201d not only solved all my access control issues, it was another link to the Exalted setting, which I\u2019m all in favor of.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Though there were some unavoidable repercussions. One, the Bane Mummies couldn\u2019t exist as my new background story for the mummies would completely preclude that. Still, the Asekh-sen are pretty nasty already and the servants of Set can create those whenever they feel like, so they\u2019ll do as stand-ins for the Bane Mummies. Two, it would also preclude the Cabiri, but I liked having them around as another occasionally-seen-in-the-shadows faction, so I found a way to include them. Having just recently got around to picking up World of Darkness: Immortals, the Purified struck me as the perfect replacement for the Cabiri. These immortals were clearly meant as a homage to the Mummy sourcebooks, but they were inferior immortals when compared to the true mummies. But, since the Cabiri were said to be the creations of \u201ca bastardized variant of the Spell of Life,\u201d that actually worked out even more perfectly. I just needed to make the casting requirements to become one of these lesser immortals difficult as well in order to justify their rarity, and there you go.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">For the true mummies, however, I wanted to stress what I believe they ought to be doing, namely combatting the darkness in the World of Darkness. This appears to be what the 3<sup>rd</sup> Edition authors were (clumsily) attempting to do via the Judges of Ma\u2019at, but I wanted a less Big Brother approach to steering mummies towards the straight and narrow. Hence, the package of Merits that ensure they can\u2019t miss the monsters lurking in the shadows, and that they can\u2019t try to make friends with those monsters. No nagging parents looking over their shoulder, but their new nature is just fundamentally incompatible with evil, so this should eventually clue them in to what side they\u2019re on.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Anyhow, hopefully this answers your questions, but I\u2019m happy to clarify any points.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">P.S. If you want to explore further links between Exalted and the World of Darkness, I recommend <a href=\"https://holdenshearer.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">this</a> not-exactly-official supplement to the World of Darkness by one of the former Exalted developers.</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":28819,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28819-arcanum-scholar","user":"Arcanum Scholar","date":". 07-07-2025, 05:26 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28819-arcanum-scholar","name":"Arcanum Scholar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"20"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-07-07T14:48:51-07:00","id":1552167,"title":"","content":"I love the Exalted crossover and I like a lot of your changes, especially with the Cabiri, but I would keep the Teomallki and the Wu T\u2019ian because I like the idea of having mummies from other ancient cultural traditions besides Egypt.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":